course: dictionary
lesson: 8958
title: Simmer: cooking, feelings and calm
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: The word simmer has more than one meaning, so context is the key. Read the words around it. «When you cook with alcohol, some of it always stays in the food; long simmering removes most but not all.» Here, simmering is a way of cooking: gently heating food in liquid, just below boiling, with small bubbles.
explain: Simmer can also describe a feeling. If anger is simmering, it stays strong under the surface and has not burst out yet. A reader who sees «simmering tension» in a story knows trouble may be coming. Ask yourself: is this about food, a feeling, or someone calming down?
explain: Simmer down is an informal phrase that means calm down. «Chill out» (slang), «simmer down» (an older phrase from cooking) and «calm down» (standard) all mean become calm. Simmer is also a noun: a recipe may say «Bring to a simmer», meaning bring the liquid to the state of gently bubbling.
fact: Simmer means to cook gently in liquid, just below boiling, with small bubbles.
fact: Water boils at 100 °C at sea level, and simmering is a little lower than that.
fact: When a feeling is simmering, it stays strong under the surface without bursting out.
fact: «Simmer down» means calm down, and it is informal.
fact: A simmer is the state of gently bubbling, as in «Bring to a simmer».
exercise: «When you cook with alcohol, some of it always stays in the food; long simmering removes most but not all.» What does simmering mean here? | Freezing very fast | *Cooking gently in liquid, with small bubbles | Cutting food into tiny pieces | Shouting loudly at the cooker
exercise: «Chill out» (slang), «simmer down» and «calm down» all mean what? | Fall fast asleep | Become hot and angry | *Become calm | Begin to boil over
exercise: A recipe says, «Bring to a simmer.» What does a simmer mean? | A loud, rolling boil | A small wooden spoon | *The state of gently bubbling | A cold, frozen block
test: «Her anger was simmering quietly all through dinner, though she said nothing.» What does simmering mean for her anger? | Turning into a song | *Staying strong, hidden under the surface | Gone completely | Bursting out in a loud shout
test: Water boils at 100 °C at sea level. Which is hotter, boiling or simmering? | Neither has any heat | Both are exactly the same | Simmering | *Boiling
exam: Which phrase means calm down? | Turn up the heat | Bring to the boil | *Simmer down | Boil over
